Reproducible Deployments with OpenShift Image Streams
Objectives
•	Ensure reproducible application deployments.
•	Use image streams and short image names to decouple deployments from specific registries.
•	Enable rollbacks and controlled updates of container images.
________________________________________
1. Image Streams Overview
•	Image streams abstract container images in OpenShift:
o	Provide stable, short names independent of registry or runtime.
o	Enable CI/CD pipelines to promote images between environments.
o	Ensure rollback to the last known-good image.
o	Track image SHA IDs for immutable reference.
•	Key distinction: floating vs non-floating tags
o	Floating: a tag like latest may point to different images over time.
o	Non-floating: image streams lock to specific SHA IDs, ensuring reproducibility.
•	OpenShift extends Kubernetes:
o	Deployment objects can reference image streams instead of direct registry images.
o	Supports automatic rollbacks via image stream tag history.
________________________________________
2. Image Stream Tags (ISTags)
•	Each image stream can have multiple tags.
•	Each tag stores:
o	Metadata about the container image (registry, name, SHA ID).
o	History of previous images for rollback.
•	Tags can pull from different registries or repositories.
•	Example of inspecting PHP image stream tags:
oc get istag -n openshift | grep php
oc describe is php -n openshift
•	Current SHA ID is marked with *.
________________________________________
3. Image Names, Tags, and SHA IDs
•	Image name: registry/repository/image:tag
•	SHA ID: unique, immutable identifier.
•	Deployments referencing a simple name (e.g., php:8.0-ubi8) may pull different images if registry changes.
•	Image streams store the SHA ID to freeze the image, enabling consistent deployments.
________________________________________
4. Use Cases
1.	Environment promotion
o	Dev → QA → Prod
o	Example:
myapp:latest-dev → myapp:qa-stable → myapp:prod-v1.2
o	Ensures tested images are used across environments.
2.	Registry flexibility
o	Swap image source if vendor image has issues.
o	Use Red Hat certified images for security and stability.
________________________________________
5. Creating and Managing Image Streams
•	Create image stream
oc create is keycloak
•	Add tags
oc import-image keycloak:25.0 --from=quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:25.0.2 --confirm
oc tag quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:25.0.2 keycloak:25.0
•	Update tag to new version
oc tag quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:25.0.3 keycloak:25.0
•	Scheduled updates
oc tag quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:25.0.3 keycloak:25.0 --scheduled
•	Pull-through from internal registry
oc tag quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:25.0.3 keycloak:25.0 --reference-policy local
________________________________________
6. Using Image Streams in Deployments
•	Enable local lookup policy
oc set image-lookup keycloak
oc describe is keycloak
oc set image-lookup keycloak --enabled=false  # disable
•	Create deployment referencing an image stream
oc create deployment mykeycloak --image keycloak:25.0
•	Works with other workload resources:
o	Jobs:
oc create job myjob --image keycloak:25.0 -- mycommand
o	CronJobs:
oc create cronjob mycron --image keycloak:25.0 --schedule "*/5 * * * *" -- mycommand
o	Pods:
oc run mypod --image keycloak:25.0
________________________________________
7. Image Stream YAML Structure
•	Key sections:
o	metadata.name → ImageStream name
o	spec.tags → desired tags and sources
o	status.dockerImageRepository → internal registry path
o	status.tags.items.dockerImageReference → SHA IDs of imported images
Example snippet:
spec:
  lookupPolicy:
    local: false
  tags:
    - name: 7.4-ubi8
      from:
        kind: DockerImage
        name: registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/ubi8/php-74:latest
      importPolicy:
        importMode: Legacy
________________________________________
8. Best Practices
•	Use image streams for reproducibility instead of direct registry images.
•	Enable local lookup policy for short names in deployments.
•	Use scheduled imports for trusted images to auto-update with security fixes.
•	Always reference SHA IDs for immutable deployments.
•	Image streams enable CI/CD integration and environment promotion without changing deployment definitions.

